[Fact]
public void DisplayNameReadsAttributesCorrectly()
{
    var appElement = Browser.MountTestComponent<DisplayNameComponent>();

    // Check that DisplayAttribute.Name is displayed
    var displayNameLabel = appElement.FindElement(By.Id("product-name-label"));
    Browser.Equal("Product Name", () => displayNameLabel.Text);

    // Check that DisplayNameAttribute is displayed
    var priceLabel = appElement.FindElement(By.Id("price-label"));
    Browser.Equal("Unit Price", () => priceLabel.Text);

    // Check that DisplayAttribute takes precedence over DisplayNameAttribute
    var stockLabel = appElement.FindElement(By.Id("stock-label"));
    Browser.Equal("Stock Quantity", () => stockLabel.Text);

    // Check fallback to property name when no attributes present
    var descriptionLabel = appElement.FindElement(By.Id("description-label"));
    Browser.Equal("Description", () => descriptionLabel.Text);

    // Check that ResourceType localization works with English resources
    var localizedLabel = appElement.FindElement(By.Id("localized-label"));
    Browser.Equal("Product Name", () => localizedLabel.Text);
}

findings.body:
`_memberInfoCache` is a process-wide `ConcurrentDictionary<Expression, MemberInfo>`. `Expression` doesn't override `Equals`/`GetHashCode`, so keys are compared **by reference**.

::code{file="src/Components/Web/src/Forms/ExpressionMemberAccessor.cs" lines="15-30"}

Razor turns `For="() => Input.Email"` into a fresh expression tree each time the parent's `BuildRenderTree` runs. `DisplayName.SetParametersAsync` sees a new reference (`For != _previousFieldAccessor` is always true after a parent render) and calls `GetDisplayName(For)`. That calls `GetOrAdd` with a brand-new key, which always misses and adds another entry.

::callout{type="perf"}
Each parent re-render of each `<DisplayName>` adds one permanently rooted entry. The entry holds the whole expression tree plus its closure, which captures the component and model instance. Entries are only evicted on a hot-reload delta, which never happens in production. On Blazor Server, a page with 5 labels and frequent re-renders (for example, every keystroke in a bound input) leaks memory and keeps disposed circuits' components alive.

The cache also gives no speedup, because no lookup ever hits. Compare `FieldIdentifier`, which caches on `(Type, MemberInfo)` rather than on the expression (::file{file="src/Components/Forms/src/FieldIdentifier.cs"}).

Fix: drop the expression-keyed cache, since parsing a single `MemberExpression` is cheap, and cache on `MemberInfo` instead:

::suggestion{file="src/Components/Web/src/Forms/ExpressionMemberAccessor.cs" lines="26-30"}
    private static MemberInfo GetMemberInfo<TValue>(Expression<Func<TValue>> accessor)
    {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(accessor);
        var accessorBody = accessor.Body;

`_displayNameCache` (line 16) is never read or written. `GetDisplayName(MemberInfo)` calls `GetCustomAttribute<DisplayAttribute>()` and `GetCustomAttribute<DisplayNameAttribute>()` on every invocation, which in practice is every parent render. `ClearCache` also doesn't clear it, so if someone later wires it up without updating `ClearCache`, hot reload will serve stale names.

::code{file="src/Components/Web/src/Forms/ExpressionMemberAccessor.cs" lines="53-86"}

::callout{type="warning"}
If you cache the localized result, you lose per-request culture switching for `[Display(ResourceType=...)]`, because `GetName()` reads the resource for the current UI culture. Either cache the `DisplayAttribute`/`DisplayNameAttribute` lookup (not the final string) or key the cache by `(MemberInfo, CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture)`.

Suggested shape:

::suggestion{file="src/Components/Web/src/Forms/ExpressionMemberAccessor.cs" lines="83-86"}
    private static void ClearCache()
    {
        _displayNameCache.Clear();
    }
